Typecasting in Python:
- The conversion of one data type into the other data type is known as typecasting or conversion in Python.
- Python supports a wide variety of functions / methods like int(), float(), str(), hex(), oct(), tuple(), set(), list(), dict(), etc. for typecasting.

Types:
1) Explicit Conversion
2) Implicit Conversion

1) Explicit Conversion -
- The conversion of one data type into another data type, done via developer or programmer's intervention or manually as per the requirement.
- Can be achieved with the help of built-in type conversion functions.

Eg.
a = "1"
b = "2"
print(int(a) + int(b))


2) Implicit Casting -
- Data types in Python do not have the same level i.e. ordering of data types is not the same in Python.
- Some of the data types have high order, and some have lower order.
- While performing any operations on variables with different data types in Python, one of the variable's data types will be changed to higher data type.
- According to the level, one data type is converted into other by the Python interpreter itself (automatically).

Eg.
a = 7
print(type(a))
b = 3.0
print(type(b))

c = a + b
print(c)
print(type(c))
